tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2106"} "Japan National Stadium, Kasumigaoka, Shinjuku, Tokyo"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/19000682"} "41 from 31 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/130204"} "Dina Asher-Smith"] " was the 2019 World Champion, had the fastest time of the year, and was considered the favorite but, in the 100, she injured a hamstring in the semi-final and did not qualify, which forced her to withdraw from the 200. In her absence the favorites were American " [:a {:href "/athletes/147109"} "Gabrielle Thomas"] ", who had run 21.61 at the US Olympic Trials, the defending champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/130722"} "Elaine Thompson-Herah"] ", who had already defended her 2016 100 metre gold medal, her Jamaican teammate, " [:a {:href "/athletes/114740"} "Shelly-Ann Fraser-Pryce"] ", and Bahamian " [:a {:href "/athletes/125366"} "Shaunae Miller-Uibo"] ". Another challenger was Namibian " [:a {:href "/athletes/145426"} "Christine Mboma"] ", who had dropped down from the 400 after World Athletics had not allowed her to compete in that event because of testosterone level abnormalities caused by a disorder of sexual development."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Fraser-Pryce won the first semi-final while, in the second, Thompson-Herah blasted 21.66. Behind her Mboma edged out Thomas, who only made the final as a time qualifier. In the final Thompson-Herah and Fraser-Pryce led out of the blocks, but Thompson-Herah had a clear lead coming off the turn. She would not be headed, finishing in the second fastest time ever, 21.53. Behind her, Thomas caught Fraser-Pryce but she was passed in turn by Mboma, who won the silver medal in 21.81, a world junior record, as Thomas took bronze in 21.87."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Thompson-Herah had become the first woman to defend her titles in both the 100 and 200 metres. A few days later, she and Fraser-Pryce would lead Jamaica to gold in the 4x100, making Thompson-Herah only the fifth woman to complete the Olympic sprint triple – 100, 200, 4x100 – after " [:a {:href "/athletes/73711"} "Fanny Blankers-Koen"] " (NED-1948), " [:a {:href "/athletes/64506"} "Betty Cuthbert"] " (AUS-1956), " [:a {:href "/athletes/77958"} "Wilma Rudolph"] " (USA-1960), and " [:a {:href "/athletes/77849"} "Florence Griffith Joyner"] " (USA-1988)."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2521"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/130722"} "Elaine Thompson-Herah"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JAM"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JAM.png"}] "JAM"]] [:td {} "22.86 (3 h6)"] [:td {} "21.66 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "21.53 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2976"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/145426"} "Christine Mboma"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NAM"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NAM.png"}] "NAM"]] [:td {} "22.11 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "21.97 (2 h2)"] [:td {} "21.81 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3893"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/147109"} "Gabby Thomas"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "22.20 (2 h4)"] [:td {} "22.01 (3 h2)"] [:td {} "21.87 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2480"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/a